from on high the helicopters descended  to collect the remains of Francisco  Franco in life he was known for his  ironclad rule who saw his own leadership  as a Catholic crusade in death he became  a relic of autocracy a figure of times  past and a reminder of a troubling era  it was a monument to a man who dominated  twentieth century Spain built on a site  that holds one of the biggest mass  graves in Europe it became known as the  valley of the Fallen constructed by the  forced labor of political prisoners at  the time  Franco's family had resisted the Spanish  government's decision to move his coffin  but despite the pressure from right-wing  groups Spain Supreme Court ruled that  the exclamation had to go ahead  i fear most' regimen it took a lot to  get rid of a repressive regime with his  tightness almost as long to get rid of  its artifice of public homage there is  room for all kinds of opinions on when  to carry this out each one with its own  criteria the government of Spain  announced it would do it in the moment  it was possible to do so this is a time  and so the remains of Francisco Franco  arrived at the cemetery  the exclamation a symbolic end of his  dictatorship and the end of Spain's  transition to democracy but even 44  years after his death there are still  repercussions being felt the decision to  remove the remains provoked a furious  reaction from Franco's family and a  reluctance to accept why for so many of  the victims families it was a chance to  turn the page in Spain's history well  you know all the victims and our victims  and their victims on both sides and  we've tried to put this in our past and  we have not been able to because of this  new present we had put this thing behind  us few of the political prisoners forced  to build the complex at the valley of  the Fallen are still alive Nicolas  Sanchez albertov was one of the only two  who managed to escape the prison there  his hope that it can be transformed into  a place of reconciliation it opens  possibility  for having more decent life in Spain and  more decent democracy because democracy  up to now has been tainted by all these  problems the memories of this turbulent  time are never far behind but the  exclamation with all its complexities  may help to reconcile Spain's torment  from this era  Sonia a go al Jazeera el pardo Madrid
 

